# /etc/input-freshness.env — config for input-freshness.sh (0600 root)
#
# Create a "Push" monitor in Uptime Kuma (uptime.owner.example) named
# "agent-box input freshness" and paste its push URL here. Until it is set, the
# check runs and journals but cannot alert — which is the same silence the check
# exists to break, so it says so loudly on every run.
#
# THE INTERVAL RULE (ORB-154, do not skip this). The monitor's heartbeat interval
# AND its retry interval must both be at least TWICE this script's timer period.
# input-freshness.timer runs hourly, so both are 7800s. Setting a window at or
# below the pusher's period makes the monitor alternate up/down forever: the two
# older guards on this box did exactly that for weeks — `agent-box compose drift`
# sent Bendik a red and a green every single hour from 4 to 19 August — and the
# cost is not the noise itself, it is that a real alert then lands somewhere no
# one is reading. Details in docs/runbooks/box-alerting.md.
KUMA_PUSH_URL=

# ACKNOWLEDGED CHECKS — space-separated `check-name:TICKET`. An acknowledged check
# still runs and still prints STALE; it just does not turn the monitor red. Only
# ever for a defect that is known AND filed, and the ticket id is mandatory so the
# ack has somewhere to go and die. Remove the entry when the ticket closes.
#
# On the MAC this must contain the iMessage rot, or the check is red from the day
# it is installed and gets learned as noise:
#   INPUT_FRESHNESS_ACK=imessage-content:ORB-162
# On the BOX it should be empty — the iMessage check skips there anyway.
INPUT_FRESHNESS_ACK=

# Optional overrides (defaults shown):
# DB_CONTAINER=agent-box-db-1
# VAULT_PATH=/srv/brain
# NETWORK_DB=/srv/network/network.db
# IMESSAGE_SOURCE_DB=$HOME/.lares/network.db
